{:edb/db-before-t 1115 :edb/db-after-t 1116 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534428 50 #inst "2026-10-11T20:38:57.747Z" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186163846 1078 #edb/ref 17592186163848 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186163858 1078 #edb/ref 17592186163863 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186163864 1078 #edb/ref 17592186163869 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186166485 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n},$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle x,\\: 2z,\\: z-y \\right\\rangle\\!, \n\\quad \n\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle z,\\: -y,\\: -x \\right\\rangle\\!,\n\\quad\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}}{\\Vert \\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}} \\Vert}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n![](images/35/35bcfa2242051bed034ac3343c1a6d67f94916d4267030f726e2bd27cbbe02f1.png)\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139534428 17592186045417 false] [17592186166485 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the surface $S$ with normal vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n},$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle x,\\: 2z,\\: z-y \\right\\rangle\\!, \n\\quad \n\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle z,\\: -y,\\: -x \\right\\rangle\\!,\n\\quad\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{\\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}}}{\\Vert \\widetilde{\\mathbf{n}} \\Vert}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ with respect to $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by\n\n![](images/41/4152d997be216db1a67b5f55ee112d1008ea7399e57f699ac62ba03654bd7fd5.svg)\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186166880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421236 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186166880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421238 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186166880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421240 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186166880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421242 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186166880 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421243 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186166881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421239 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186166881 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421244 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186166882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167131 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186166882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421235 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186166882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421237 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186166882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421241 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186166981 1050 1140.0 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186167128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186167128 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨z, y, x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z = 1$ for $0 \\le x \\le 1$ and $0 \\le y \\le 1$. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n$." 13194139534428 17592186045417 false] [17592186167128 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186167128 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=1$ for  $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ the upward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186167130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186167130 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨x, y, z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z = 2x + 2y - 2$ for $0 \\le x \\le 3$ and $-1 \\le y \\le 1$. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n$.\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $\\mid \\mid r_{x}^{′} \\cdot r_{y}^{′} \\mid |= 3$.*" 13194139534428 17592186045417 false] [17592186167130 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=2x+2y-2$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $-1\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=3.$*" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186167130 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=2x+2y-2$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-2,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-2,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=2.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2x+2y-2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,\\,y,\\,2x+2y-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x+2y-(2x+2y-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}}\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186167131 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186167131 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x, y, z) = ⟨x, y, z + 3⟩$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z = x + y$ such that $Area(S) = \\sqrt{3}$. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n$." 13194139534428 17592186045417 false] [17592186167131 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z+3⟩$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=x+y$ such that $\\text{Area}(S)=\\sqrt{3}.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186167131 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z+3⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x,\\,y,\\,x+y+3⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-x-y+(x+y+3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186168146 1064 "In three dimensions, flux measures the total flow of a vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ *through an orientable surface.*\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is a three-dimensional vector field, and $S$ is an orientable surface with the choice of orientation given by the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n(x,y,z),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/9976f35dcf348da89c15a40aad43ab5a1d3edea75558ef5539f9421ed9b75f4e.png)\n\n \n\nThe **flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $\\boldsymbol{S}$** with respect to $\\mathbf n,$ which we write as \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf S\n$$\n\n is given by the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S  \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n \\,\\textrm d S.\n$$\n\nIt's worth taking a moment to compare this to the flux formula for two-dimensional vector fields: \n\n- The quantity $\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n$ evaluated at a point $P$ on $S$ measures the projection of $\\mathbf F$ in the direction of $\\mathbf n$ at $P.$\n\n- By summing (integrating) the quantity $\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n$ over the entire surface, we get the total flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$\n\nOur flux depends on our choice of orientation. If we were to select the *opposite* orientation, we'd get a result with the same magnitude but opposite sign." 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186168146 1064 "In three dimensions, flux measures the total flow of a vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ *through an orientable surface.*\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ is a three-dimensional vector field, and $S$ is an orientable surface with the choice of orientation given by the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n(x,y,z),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/9976f35dcf348da89c15a40aad43ab5a1d3edea75558ef5539f9421ed9b75f4e.png)\n\nThe **flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $\\boldsymbol{S}$** with respect to $\\mathbf n,$ which we write as \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf S\n$$\n\n is given by the surface integral\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S  \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n \\,\\textrm d S.\n$$\n\nIt's worth taking a moment to compare this to the flux formula for two-dimensional vector fields: \n\n- The quantity $\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n$ evaluated at a point $P$ on $S$ measures the projection of $\\mathbf F$ in the direction of $\\mathbf n$ at $P.$\n\n- By summing (integrating) the quantity $\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n$ over the entire surface, we get the total flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$\n\nOur flux depends on our choice of orientation. If we were to select the *opposite* orientation, we'd get a result with the same magnitude but opposite sign.\n\nAs in the case of two-dimensional vector fields, we have a choice between two possible normal vectors at each point. For instance, we could have picked the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction, as shown below.\n\n<div id=\"graphic-b46ee2d2f4637027c6704e244e0b2f96\" class=\"graphic\"><img src=\"/graphics/b46ee2d2f4637027c6704e244e0b2f96\" /></div> \n\nThe key takeaway is that when calculating the flux of a vector field across a surface, we must be clear about which orientation we're measuring our flux relative to." 13194139534428 17592186045417 false] [17592186168147 1064 "A surface is **orientable** if it has two distinct sides. So, for example, the surface $S$ shown below is orientable.\n\n![](images/29/29be1e415eaf0072aea7f4093e71665f5996b29804702a37a4d4493e0b0002d4.png)\n\n \n\nIf a unit vector $\\mathbf n(x,y,z)$ to our orientable surface varies continuously over $S,$ we say that $S$ is **oriented** and the choice of $\\mathbf n$ gives an **orientation** of $S.$\n\nWe can choose the opposite orientation for our surface by selecting a vector that points in the opposite direction, as shown below.\n\n![](images/40/40268c448b7c4ac99acf356e9f1e2702112a0d46dc1b6e6afd98d5c95e5ea317.png)\n\n \n\nWhen dealing with two-sided (i.e., orientable) surfaces, we must always be clear about our chosen orientation. \n\nNote that some surfaces are *not* orientable. This means they do not have two distinct sides. An example of a non-orientable surface is a Möbius strip, shown below.\n\n![](images/27/277ffb9e1594e64f3411b0c5063841616aaa433ccc4915bc96e2c9ab865ece95.png)\n\n \n\nA Möbius strip can be made by taking a long rectangular piece of paper, twisting one of its ends by $180^\\circ$ and gluing the two ends together. \n\nA Möbius strip does not have two distinct sides because if you were to start at a point $P$ on the strip and move along it (following the dark blue curve shown), you'd eventually end up at the same point but on the opposite side! Thus, a Möbius strip only has one distinct side. \n\nIn the work that follows, we will only consider orientable surfaces." 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186168147 1064 "A surface is **orientable** if it has two distinct sides. So, for example, the surface $S$ shown below is orientable.\n\n![](images/29/29be1e415eaf0072aea7f4093e71665f5996b29804702a37a4d4493e0b0002d4.png)\n\nIf a unit vector $\\mathbf n(x,y,z)$ to our orientable surface varies continuously over $S,$ we say that $S$ is **oriented** and the choice of $\\mathbf n$ gives an **orientation** of $S.$\n\nWe can choose the opposite orientation for our surface by selecting a vector that points in the opposite direction, as shown below.\n\n![](images/40/40268c448b7c4ac99acf356e9f1e2702112a0d46dc1b6e6afd98d5c95e5ea317.png)\n\nWhen dealing with two-sided (i.e., orientable) surfaces, we must always be clear about our chosen orientation. \n\nNote that some surfaces are *not* orientable. This means they do not have two distinct sides. An example of a non-orientable surface is a Möbius strip, shown below.\n\n![](images/27/277ffb9e1594e64f3411b0c5063841616aaa433ccc4915bc96e2c9ab865ece95.png)\n\nA Möbius strip can be made by taking a long rectangular piece of paper, twisting one of its ends by $180^\\circ$ and gluing the two ends together. \n\nA Möbius strip does not have two distinct sides because if you were to start at a point $P$ on the strip and move along it (following the dark blue curve shown), you'd eventually end up at the same point but on the opposite side! Thus, a Möbius strip only has one distinct side. \n\nIn the work that follows, we will only consider orientable surfaces." 13194139534428 17592186045417 false] [17592186168148 1064 "Suppose we have the horizontal plane $z=k,$ where $k$ is a fixed parameter. \n\nThe magnitude of the fundamental vector product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}|| & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂k}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂k}{∂y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we have that\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf r_x =  \\langle 1, 0, 0\\rangle = \\mathbf i, \\qquad \\mathbf r_y =  \\langle 0, 1, 0\\rangle = \\mathbf j.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSo, the fundamental vector product is given by\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf r_x \\times\\mathbf r_y = \\mathbf i\\times\\mathbf j = \\mathbf k\n\\]\n\nand therefore\n\n\\[\n|| \\mathbf r_x \\times\\mathbf r_y|| = ||\\mathbf k|| =1. \n\\]\n\nTherefore, in such cases, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,f(x,y,z)\\text{d}S\\, & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRestating, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z)\\, \\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y,z)  \\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nWe arrive at a similar result when considering vertical planes parallel to the $xz$- or $yz$-axes.\n\nThis observation can help provide a shortcut when calculating the flux of a vector field through a horizontal plane. Let's see an example." 13194139534428 17592186045417 false] [17592186168148 1064 "Suppose we have the horizontal plane $z=k,$ where $k$ is a fixed parameter. \n\nThe magnitude of the fundamental vector product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}|| & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂k}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂k}{∂y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in such cases, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,f(x,y,z)\\text{d}S\\, & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRestating, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z)\\, \\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y,z)  \\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nWe arrive at a similar result when considering vertical planes parallel to the $xz$- or $yz$-axes.\n\nThis observation can help provide a shortcut when calculating the flux of a vector field through a horizontal plane. Let's see an example." 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421175 1080 "96" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421175 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421175 1083 #uuid "03158da1-b1d2-5e08-9fb3-bcf52d0b7ade" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421176 1080 "3{x}^{3}+yz" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421176 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421176 1083 #uuid "07b10f22-95ea-52eb-9611-ba4dd8792428" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421177 1080 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421177 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421177 1083 #uuid "118324c2-6877-51fd-9281-af0fdd0a2878" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421178 1080 "-48" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421178 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421178 1083 #uuid "228c1e6f-6068-550e-b050-12aa6caa4351" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421179 1080 "3{x}^{2}-yz" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421179 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421179 1083 #uuid "25f30ee3-665e-5e1b-aa22-c26247f952a9" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421180 1080 "x+2y-z" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421180 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421180 1083 #uuid "2de3a55c-a3c2-5a9a-a812