nce we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x-4y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,x+1,\\,-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}⟨x-4y+1,\\,x+1,\\,-y⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-4,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}(x-4y+1-4(x+1)+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}(-3x-3y-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-x-y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-x-y-1)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(-x-y-1)\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}}(-x-y-1)\\cdot 3\\cancel{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-3{∬}_{D}(x+y+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+y+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+xy+x]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2y+4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-3[{y}^{2}+4y]_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 32 \\\\  & =-96\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186421228 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421236 1069 "q-161202" 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534428 17592186045417 true] [17592186421236 1071 "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit 